From: Herman van Hazendonk <github.com@herrie.org>
Date: Fri, 19 Sep 2026 00:00:00 +0200
Subject: [PATCH] arm64: dts: rockchip: pinetab2: give vdd_cpu a settling margin

The PineTab 2 resets - a silent power-on reset, nothing in the kernel log,
CRU_GLB_RST_ST clear afterwards - whenever the CPU frequency is changed
while a NEON-heavy load is running (libcamera's software ISP is the
reliable trigger: five-frame capture cycles reset the tablet within a
handful of iterations under schedutil, at 1.8 GHz, capped at 1.416 GHz or
floored at 1.416 GHz alike, but survive dozens of cycles with the
frequency pinned; sustained or bursty load without frequency changes is
fine).

vdd_cpu is a TCS4525 with regulator-ramp-delay = <2300>, and the OPP core
waits exactly delta_uV / 2300 us after commanding a new voltage before it
raises the clock. That leaves no margin at all for the regulator's own
latency and slew tolerance, so under a heavy load the cores are clocked up
before the rail has arrived. Wait a fixed 300 us on the way up instead
(the largest step, 0.825 to 1.15 V, nominally takes 141 us).

Upstream-Status: Pending

Signed-off-by: Herman van Hazendonk <github.com@herrie.org>
---
--- a/arch/arm64/boot/dts/rockchip/rk3566-pinetab2.dtsi
+++ b/arch/arm64/boot/dts/rockchip/rk3566-pinetab2.dtsi
@@ -381,6 +381,13 @@
 		regulator-min-microvolt = <800000>;
 		regulator-max-microvolt = <1150000>;
 		regulator-ramp-delay = <2300>;
+		/*
+		 * The ramp delay alone gives the OPP core no margin between
+		 * the voltage arriving and the clock going up, and the SoC
+		 * resets under load when the clock wins. Settle first.
+		 */
+		regulator-settling-time-up-us = <300>;
+		regulator-settling-time-down-us = <300>;
 		regulator-always-on;
 		regulator-boot-on;
 		vin-supply = <&vcc_sys>;
